## Intuition
The main idea is to combine intervals that overlap so that each merged interval represents a continuous range.
If we sort intervals by their starting time, then any overlapping intervals will appear next to each other. We can then iterate through the list and merge intervals whenever the next interval starts before or at the end of the current one.

---

## Approach
1. Sort the intervals based on their starting values.

1. Initialize an empty list to store the merged intervals.

1. Traverse the sorted intervals:

    - Start with the current interval’s start and end.

    - Keep extending the end as long as the next interval overlaps (i.e., its start is less than or equal to the current end).

1. Once no more overlaps are found, add the merged interval to the result list.

1. Continue until all intervals are processed.

---

## Solution
```java[]
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> mergeOverlap(List<List<Integer>> intervals) {
        List<List<Integer>> ans = new ArrayList<>();

        int n = intervals.size();

        Collections.sort(intervals, (a, b) -> a.get(0) - b.get(0));

        List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < n; ) {
            list = new ArrayList<>();

            int minVal = intervals.get(i).get(0);
            int maxVal = intervals.get(i).get(1);

            list.add(minVal);

            int idx = i + 1;

            while (idx < n && intervals.get(idx).get(0) <= maxVal) {
                maxVal = Math.maxVal(maxVal, intervals.get(idx).get(1));
                idx++;
            }

            i = idx;
            list.add(maxVal);

            ans.add(new ArrayList(list));
        }

        return ans;
    }
}
